# =============================================================================
# Docker Build Context Exclusions - Repository Root
# =============================================================================
# Docker loads the ignore file from the BUILD CONTEXT ROOT only, as
# <context>/.dockerignore, and from nowhere else. Both build paths in this
# repository set the context to the repository root:
#
#   .github/workflows/cd.yml            context: .       -> repository root
#   infrastructure/docker/docker-compose.yml
#                                       context: ../..   -> repository root
#
# So this file is what actually filters every build here. The older
# infrastructure/docker/.dockerignore sits BELOW both context roots, is never
# loaded by any build path, and is kept only as a record of intent.
#
# HOW THESE PATTERNS MATCH: paths are matched RELATIVE TO THE CONTEXT ROOT,
# which is the repository root - not relative to the Dockerfile's directory.
# A bare pattern such as `node_modules/` therefore matches only a top-level
# directory and would NOT match src/backend/node_modules. Everything this file
# needs to exclude lives one or two levels down, which is why the recursive
# `**/` form below is required rather than optional.
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT EXCLUDED, because the build reads them from the context:
#   src/backend/package.json, src/backend/package-lock.json  (dependency install)
#   src/backend/{server,app}.js and config/ middleware/ routes/ utils/  (sources)
#
# The Dockerfile named with `-f` and this ignore file are always sent to the
# daemon regardless of the rules below, so excluding infrastructure/ is safe.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dependency directories
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This is the most important exclusion in the file. The runtime stage installs
# production-only dependencies with `npm ci --omit=dev` and then copies the
# application sources on top of that install. If a developer's local
# src/backend/node_modules reached the build context, it would travel into the
# builder stage and that copy would overwrite the production tree with a
# development one - a larger image carrying test and tooling packages it must
# never ship. The recursive form is what prevents it at the source.
**/node_modules

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Test coverage output and log files
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated locally by `npm test` (coverage is collected on every run) and by
# the running process. Build output, never build input.
**/coverage
**/*.log

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Development tooling configuration
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Test runner, file watcher and code-style configuration are development-only.
# The production image runs `npm start` and reads none of them, so shipping
# them would only widen the image and its attack surface.
**/jest.config.js
**/nodemon.json
**/.eslintrc.js
**/.prettierrc

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Environment files
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local configuration must never be baked into an image; the container is
# configured through environment variables at run time instead.
# `**/.env` matches the literal filename `.env` and does NOT match
# `.env.example`, so the template needs its own pattern. It is excluded because
# the running image never reads it - src/backend/config/index.js loads `.env`
# via dotenv, and the template is a learner artifact with no runtime role.
**/.env
**/.env.example

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tests
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The suites run locally and in CI against the source tree, not inside the
# production image. Path-qualified because this is the only tests directory.
src/backend/tests

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Version control
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Repository history is large, changes on every commit (needlessly
# invalidating build cache) and has no runtime purpose.
.git

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Documentation and infrastructure definitions
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tutorial documentation and the container, Compose and Kubernetes definitions
# describe how to build and deploy the application; the application itself does
# not read them.
docs/
infrastructure/
